## By the Numbers: A Surging Recall Trend

This year alone, we've seen a staggering 807 recalls, a 16% increase from last year's total at the same time. Class II recalls make up the bulk with 478 cases, though the 234 Class I incidents remain the most concerning. Among these, repeat offenders like D'Dioses Fruit Pops and Spring & Mulberry Inc. continue to highlight issues within certain brands.

## The Culprit of the Day: D'Dioses Fruit Pops

D'Dioses Fruit Pops, a repeat offender, has issued multiple Class I recalls within the last week. The company pulled numerous ice pop varieties off shelves due to undeclared milk, pecans, pistachios, and artificial dyes Yellow #5 and Red #40--posing significant risks to allergy sufferers and those avoiding artificial additives.

### Details of the Recall:
1. Ice Pop, D'Dioses Nuez (UPC 827912008548)
2. Ice Pop, D'Dioses Tamarindo (UPC 827912008593)
3. Ice Pop, D'Dioses Arroz con Leche (UPC 710594511843)

...and more, totaling 12 distinct products that failed to declare their contents properly. This marks D'Dioses Fruit Pops as one of the year's worst offenders with 16 recalls, 14 of which are Class I.

## Why This Matters:

Undeclared allergens and additives not only threaten public safety but also highlight systemic issues in labeling and manufacturing processes. Given this surge in recalls, consumers must remain vigilant and check product labels and recall announcements regularly.

## Other Recalls of Note:
- Nutricia North America issued a Class II recall for subpotent Vitamin A levels in their Liquid Hope meal replacement.
- Fayus Inc. recalled OLA-OLA Pounded Groundnuts due to undeclared peanuts.
- Gellert Global Group found glass in their Ruby Artichoke Hearts.

## Verdict:

Avoid D'Dioses Fruit Pops products as a precautionary measure. Their track record for undisclosed ingredients is troubling.

## Brands to Watch:

Top offenders include D'Dioses Fruit Pops, Wellnov Supplements LLC, and Spring & Mulberry Inc. Their recall history warrants consumer skepticism.
